INSERT INTO feature VALUES('check-memo','kernel','Check memoization',NULL,'implemented','notable',NULL,'A checker skips a question already answered in the run, on both machines by default, keyed by the content table''s name for the goal: exactly the binders it reads, each with its type, as one goal record that holds each read binder''s index and its type''s name inline, each type named once and shared — the hereditarily finite set reading of a support (#866: a judgement served wherever its dependency set recurs in the run; the fixture −3.4 % instructions, recalls 5 629 → 5 994). A table made to name its records gives each record a name that two sessions give alike: BLAKE3 over its tag, its payload and its members'' names, never their ids, under the key of its identification; a goal''s name is its goal record''s. The name locates a record and never decides: two records under one name keep their own ids. A constant named by its admission position names a goal alike only under one admitted prefix (#3001). Placement reads dense per-family columns, and interning, the memo, the reach caches and the rewrite memo probe one open-addressing hash index (#2513). A record is `u32` words, compared and hashed a word at a time (#3600). The content table and its names live as long as the environment (#3549). The session''s table names each record it admits. The bridge''s fold names each image there when it mints it, after its children, so a key finds each bridge image named and places only the nodes the kernel minted. The kernel names a node it minted on first demand, once for the environment''s life. A plain definition''s digest is the digest of the names of its declared type and its body, so its naming walks nothing, and the table compares each twin. A truncation of the arena retires the names of the nodes that it drops. Beneath each declaration''s memo, the environment keeps the content outcomes of its earlier admissions (#2800). A content outcome is a check that succeeded or a level that a type formed at. Each is kept under its judgement''s parameters. The parameters are the level context, the manifest, the laws, the block by position and signature content, the fixpoints and the partiality. A judgement in a mutual block does not ask or keep. A judgement with relating endpoints does not ask or keep. A key reads a plain definition''s reference by its content rather than its admission position (`content-addressed-references`). The core checker is a third machine. Its run answers a value goal that it meets again from its goal memo, in one transition. A value goal checks or synthesises a value node, or forms a value type. Its key is the node, the expected type and the type of each binder that the node reads.','kernel-check-memo kernel-core kernel-core-base kernel-term core-checker','crates/kernel-check-memo/tests/differential.rs crates/kernel-check-memo/src/table.rs crates/kernel-core/tests/acceptance.rs crates/kernel-core-base/src/encoding.rs crates/kernel-core-base/src/support.rs crates/kernel-core/src/environment.rs crates/kernel-term/src/arena.rs crates/core-checker-base/src/judgement/memo/tests.rs crates/surface-dispatcher/tests/corpus.rs','corpus/model/records/iterated-pair.gandr','#3001');
